I built a tty only version under OS 10.2 that should work OK. I've 
uploaded to my public idisk folder (mhjohnson). I put the instructions 
up previously (2003), but here's a brief recap.

To get a copy using OS X use the following steps in the finder:
   Go -> Connect to Server
   enter http://idisk.mac.com/mhjohnson-Public
   Connect
I moved the Nethack 3.4.1 code into a subfolder. There's now a nh343 
folder as well with a copy of my games directory w/ nethack 3.4.3.

Create a user games (if you don't already have one).
In  terminal window as root,
    cd /usr
    tar xzf nh343.tgz
which will create the games directory if it does not exist or add to
that directory if you do. The files added are...
    games/nethack
    games/lib/nethackdir...
You'll get a couple bones and a save file for 501maniac that you can 
delete (or play if interested - its a pretty good tourist, has the PYEC, 
a ton of $$, big cache on DL 6 or so, needs to identify everything / fix 
up before visiting Medusa).

My .login file has...
    setenv NETHACKDIR /usr/games/lib/nethackdir
    setenv NETHACKOPTIONS
"autodig,\!autopickup,catname:Vladimir,disclose:+i+c+v+g+a,dogname:Quinn,fruit:jellybean,gender:M,horsename:Trigger,\!legacy,number_pad,role:M,showexp,showscore,time,DECgraphics,\!sparkle,hilite_pet"
if you want a color terminal (using the Terminal application), add
    setenv TERM dtterm
or with xterm,
    setenv TERM xterm-color
and then set reverse video.

The new version doesn't screw up the display like my previous one.

I also made some instructions on how I built nethack. They follow. If 
someone else can do these steps, I was thinking of sending them to the 
devteam so they can add another README file to the unix directory.

How To Build Nethack 3.4.3 under Mac OSX

Mark Johnson (maniac)
July 21, 2004

For reference see:
   http://www.nethack.org/
for the source of Nethack, pre built binaries, etc.

This walks through building a version using the terminal
interface. This versions is not [currently] provided at
www.nethack.org. These steps also assume you have a copy of the
development tools installed. I use specific tools such as emacs,
more, and so on - if you are comfortable with other tools, such
as vi or pico, by all means use them.

[0] Download a copy of the source tar file.

[1] Open a terminal (I prefer an xterm) window. The command lines
below should be typed into that window. They will be preceeded by a
pair of spaces.

[2] Unpack the tar file using something like:
   tar xzf nethack-343-src.tgz
this should create a sub directory named nethack-3.4.3.

[3] Go into the new directory and view README for a summary of the
steps involved.
   cd nethack-3.4.3
   more README
[or whatever application you prefer to view files with]

[4] Go into the unix subdirectory and view the installation
instructions.
   cd sys/unix
   more Install.unx
There are basically nine (9) steps listed here and we'll go through
each one with specifics for Mac OSX. Reading this file is step 0.

[5] For the most part, read step 1 but don't do anything. Unless
you've moved some files before this point, the files are all in the
correct locations. If you did move some stuff, I recommend starting
over with the unpack step (#2) above.

[6] Run the setup script
   sh setup.sh
to move the makefiles into the proper locations. This is step 2.

[7] Edit the configuration include files to match what you want. This
is step 3. I did the following:
   cd ../../include
   emacs config.h
(section 1 - confirm UNIX is defined, confirm TTY_GRAPHICS is defined).
(section 2 - I left this section alone)
(section 3 - I left this section alone)
(section 4 - I left this alone, but you may want to set various
  options for the game. Just comment out the features you don't want)
(section 5 - I left this alone, but you might want to enable
  AUTOPICKUP_EXCEPTIONS)
   emacs unixconf.h
(I viewed the file and then left it alone)

[8] Optionally, review system.h. This is step 4.

[9] Optionally update topten.c. This is step 5.
   cd ../src
   emacs topten.c
I left it alone but you may want to increase the number of "top ten"
listings for your name.

[10] Update the makefiles and build the executable. This is step 6.
According to the instructions you should do
   emacs Makefile
(You could change the compiler options, I left them alone.
  I changed WINTTYLIB to -ltermcap.
  Stop where indicated and save the update)
   cd ../util
   emacs Makefile
(I made no changes, but if you made compiler option changes, make
them here as well)
   touch makedefs.c
   cd ../src
   make
and then go get a cup of coffee or take a nap....
   "nethack is up to date."
should be the last message you see on the display. If this does not
work, need to review the changes made in this and previous steps and
try again.

[11] Edit the top level makefile and install the application. This is
step 7. If you have a previous version [like I do], you may want to
move it before doing the install. Yoy may also need to create a
"games" account if you have not done so previously as well.
   cd ..
   emacs Makefile
(I made no changes, but if you need to, do so here)
   make all
   sudo make install
At this point, there's a message indicating you should update the
documents as well, but I reviewed that makefile and other than
copying the stuff by hand, its not clear what should be done [bug?].

[12] Step 8 in the instructions is to review how to run recover. It
should also be installed from previous steps.

[13] Not explicitly a step, but you should set up your nethack
options using one of three methods:
  - setenv NETHACKOPTIONS "(string here)"
  - create ~/.nethackrc
  - create ~/Library/Preferences/Nethack Defaults.txt
(the last one has a space in the filename...)
The guidebook (doc/Guidebook) has instructions for doing this.

Another hint - if using xterm and want color / line art, try setting
   color
   DECgraphics
in your nethack options and set inverse video (black background /
white text in xterm (Ctrl-Middle Click to get menu).

At this point try running the program using
   /usr/games/nethack
and enjoy your new version of Nethack.

Good luck.
   --Mark


